# Independent concrete AES-256 review

No blocking finding in the reviewed circuit or its clean XOR interface. This
review concerns the public Boolean/gate construction under the pinned IR; it
does not establish AES as an ideal permutation, a quantum oracle, a PRP security
reduction, native execution correspondence or physical resource calibration.
No implementer files, tracked files or Rust sources were edited.

Reviewed implementation SHA-256:
`d81dc27d120b759260a07e51479f56d621d1f8e771431096ad7c7ffc8f0be3e2`.
Pinned IR SHA-256:
`f36158489182058a4742843b458d9285e11901b7714b503b42d4c940feb4cc64`.
The local FIPS 197 update-1 PDF and extracted text have identities
`62c86eb567f13edb8f71826e985da870b04ef6381634f303cdb16e84d47becd1`
and `9d75ba19894ad2b2ff3aa21a2663f9413d0d6fe3e6822cf52864b17e5663429f`.

The S-box chain computes exponents 2,3,6,12,15,30,60,120,240,252,254.
Its four polynomial products and seven linear squarings implement inversion in
the AES field; the zero case stays zero before the affine map. The affine bit
offsets and constant match FIPS 197 equation 5.3. Byte order is low-bit first
within each source byte, while the state is column major. ShiftRows and the
forward MixColumns matrix have the specified orientation. Key expansion emits
60 words, including the AES-256-specific SubWord at index 4 modulo 8. The
cipher includes the initial key addition, thirteen full rounds and the final
round without MixColumns.

Independent structural accounting gives 276 S-boxes: 224 in cipher rounds and
52 in key expansion. Each contributes 256 AND, 431 XOR and four NOT nodes.
The remaining key/round/matrix operations contribute 10,864 XOR nodes, and Rcon
adds seven NOT nodes. Thus the complete forward IR has 70,656 AND, 129,820 XOR
and 1,111 NOT nodes, occupying 201,587 fresh history bits. There are no kernel
or oracle nodes inside this AES block.

Compute/copy/uncompute emits exactly 2,222 X, 521,630 CX and 141,312 CCX gates,
with 128 copy gates included. Under the IR's stated five-elementary-gate CCX
convention this is 1,230,412 elementary gates and zero base XOR-oracle calls.
The interface needs 384 input bits, 128 separate output bits and 201,587 initially
zero history bits. Public wire relabeling is not secret-address memory access.

The separately owned `test_independent_aes.py` contains 13 controls, all passed
in 2.10 seconds with Python bytecode and pytest cache/plugins disabled. It uses
polynomial extended Euclid rather than the circuit's exponentiation chain,
big-endian integer key words, a row-major integer cipher reference and an
independent packed-basis X/CX/CCX interpreter. All 60 words of the pinned NIST
Appendix A.3 schedule agree. One streamed circuit simultaneously checks 16
independent inputs and arbitrary output targets, exact emitted counts, input
preservation, untouched neighboring wires and zero returned workspace. The
missing-uncompute control produces correct ciphertext while leaving dirty
history, demonstrating why output-only checks do not establish cleanup.
Malformed input-width/type/bit controls refuse, and input pins are checked again
after the suite. The implementer's suite separately covers relocation, repeated
clean-map inversion, alias/width/container failures and NIST known answers.

These are finite classical controls plus source-level algebra/IR reasoning.
They do not simulate arbitrary entangled states. The XOR gate construction's
linear extension follows from its reversible basis permutation under the stated
zero-work premise; no empirical quantum claim is made.
